:Of the few known Cambrian fossils that are usually accepted as chondrophorines, ''Rotadiscus grandis'' Sun and Hou, 1987, from the Chengjiang fauna in Yunnan, China, is easily distinguished by several characters.  It has an almost centrally located apex and its surface is not entirely smooth, due to small pneumatocysts.  As a result, radial structures are insignificant and concentric undulations are much more conspicuous and comparitively regular.  The outline seems to be almost circular and there is no indication of a radial depression as in ''Microschedia''.  It should be noted, however, that the figured specimens (Sun and Hou, 1987, Pl. III) are unlikely to possess a trilete central structure as stated by Sun (1991).  The structure interpreted as the mouth by Sun and Hou (1987) may represent an attachment area, which would support studies in progress by Conway Morris that shift ''Rotadiscus'' to a systematic position close to ''Eldonia'' from the Middle Cambrian Burgess Shale fauna (see Dzik, 1991).

