:There are no signs of any pentameral or tetrameral symmetry in the anatomy of ''Eldonia'', nor is there any reason to suppose the presence of a mesodermal calcitic skeleton; so both the holothurian and scyphozoan affinities of this animal are difficult to support.  A U-shaped intestine, lophophore, and an ectodermal, marginally accreted skeleton are features of the lophophorates.  Such an attribution was proposed for the eldonioids (in the rank of class) by Dzik (1991).  The anatomical data on ''R. guizhouensis'' are consistent with those on the Burgess Shale and Chengjiang species, which shows that the body plan of these organisms was rather uniform.  The relatively simple organization of this species, with a strictly U-shaped digestive tract and the disc inferred to be more conical than other species, may reflect its primitiveness with respect to the larger and more complex eldonioids.

:Different morphologies are represented in each of these localilties; but all three-dimensionally preserved eldonioids show growth lines at least on the surface of their discs.  In ''Eldonia'' (Chen ''et al.'' 1995a, 1995b) the disc was probably only lightly sclerotized, but cuticle accretion was clearly marginal at both ventral and dorsal sides of the body.
:The accretional mode of growth implies a certain stiffness of the cuticle and, even if it was not mineralized and thereby flexible in ''Eldonia'', its elasticity was definitely low. [stuff about how floating around like a jellyfish is unlikely because of this and other features].
:There is no particular reason to believe that ''Eldonia'' was different in this respect from other genre of the Eldonioidea that have basically the same body form, but differ mostly in the degree of sclerotization (and preservation) of their body cover.  The stiff skeleton of ''Velumbrella'' from Poland fragmented into angular pieces, without any plastic deformation, in a coarse sandstone matrix (Dzik 1991, fig. 3B; Masiak and Żylińska 1994); the Chengjiang ''Rotadiscus'' 'formed cracks on compaction when compared with wrinkling and folding observed in ''Eldonia''' (Chen ''et al.''). [bit about ''R.'' repairing broken disc from predation, unlikley in 'medusoid' way of life].

:''Rotadiscus'' is the most heaviliy sclerotized eldoniod, although its cuticle was probably not mineralized. [stuff about epibionts].

